9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B. The strongest case remains that Dilp2 secreted into the hemolymph is the functional source driving NSC reactivation. Fat-body Imp-L2 overexpression sequesters circulating Dilp2 and fully prevents exit from quiescence even though the IPC-to-DRN retrograde route is untouched; that single manipulation cleanly isolates the hemolymph pool as necessary. The prompt’s explicit caveat that DRNs “may have function independent of Dilp2 absorption” further decouples the retrograde-transport route from the reactivation signal itself, so the DRN-inhibition phenotype does not establish that retrogradely delivered Dilp2 is required.  

The glial Imp-L2 result is consistent with this picture because it secondarily lowers IPC Dilp2 expression (thereby shrinking the hemolymph pool) rather than proving a separate local source. The non-physiological bovine-insulin rescue on unfed brains simply confirms that NSCs can respond once an insulin-like ligand crosses the BBB, reinforcing that the circulating pool is sufficient once it reaches the brain.  

Colleagues arguing E correctly note that Imp-L2 can cross the BBB, yet the fat-body experiment still produces a complete, feeding-independent block while neuronal transport remains intact; that outcome is difficult to reconcile with a model in which the retrogradely transported pool is the primary driver. The data therefore converge on B as the operative source.}

